##:
sys/process -- the process module.

The functions of this file call the C library through an `unsafe external` block: `exit()`,
`getpid()`, `getuid()`, `chdir()` and `getcwd()`, and for `run` the functions
`posix_spawnp()` and `waitpid()` and two `tmpfile()` streams.

`chdir` and `getcwd` give `FileError` on a failure, with the variant from `errno()`
(`FileError.from_errno` of `<io/error>`). This module re-exports `<io/error>`, so
`use <sys/process>` alone can name `FileError`. `run` gives `ProcessError` when the
program does not start or a signal stops it.
:##

public use <io/error>
use <sys/platform>

unsafe external "C" as libc because "the exit, the ids, the working directory and the child processes of the C library":
    fn exit(i32 code) ! = "exit"
    fn malloc(i64 n) ptr = "malloc"
    fn free(ptr p) ~ = "free"
    fn strdup(string s) ptr = "strdup"
    fn getpid() i32 = "getpid"
    fn getuid() i32 = "getuid"
    fn chdir(string path) i32 = "chdir"
    fn getcwd(ptr buf, i64 size) Maybe@(string) = "getcwd"
    fn tmpfile() Maybe@(ptr) = "tmpfile"
    fn fileno(ptr stream) i32 = "fileno"
    fn fclose(ptr stream) i32 = "fclose"
    fn fseek(ptr stream, i64 offset, i32 whence) i32 = "fseek"
    fn ftell(ptr stream) i64 = "ftell"
    fn fread(poke u8[] buf, i64 size, i64 count, ptr stream) i64 = "fread"
    fn posix_spawn_file_actions_init(ptr actions) i32 = "posix_spawn_file_actions_init"
    fn posix_spawn_file_actions_adddup2(ptr actions, i32 fd, i32 newfd) i32 = "posix_spawn_file_actions_adddup2"
    fn posix_spawn_file_actions_destroy(ptr actions) i32 = "posix_spawn_file_actions_destroy"
    fn posix_spawnp(ptr pid, string file, ptr actions, Maybe@(ptr) attrs, ptr argv, Maybe@(ptr) envp) i32 = "posix_spawnp"
    fn waitpid(i32 pid, ptr status, i32 options) i32 = "waitpid"
    ##: The environment of this process, the `envp` of `posix_spawnp`. :##
    var Maybe@(ptr) environ = "environ"

##:
Why `run` did not give the output of a program.

A program that exits with a code that is not 0 is no error: the code is in
`ProcessOutput.exit_code`.
:##
public error ProcessError:
    ##: The program did not start: the command is not on `PATH`, or no capture stream opened. :##
    SpawnFailed
    ##: A signal stopped the program. :##
    SignalReceived

##:
What a program that `run` started gave back when it exited.
:##
public struct ProcessOutput:
    ##: The exit code of the program, from 0 to 255. :##
    i32 exit_code
    ##: All the bytes that the program wrote to its standard output. :##
    string stdout_text
    ##: All the bytes that the program wrote to its standard error. :##
    string stderr_text

##: The bytes of the opaque `posix_spawn_file_actions_t`, more than each platform needs. :##
const i64 FILE_ACTIONS_BYTES = 128

##:
Ends the program with an exit code. The call never returns.

The C library flushes the open streams, so the output that the program printed before the
call is written. No drop runs after the call: the system takes back the memory of the
process.

- Parameter code: The exit code of the process. The system keeps the low 8 bits.
- Example:
  ```sushi no_run
  if (getpid() < 0):
      exit(1)
  ```
  A function marked `!` that ends with `exit` needs no `return` after the call.
:##
public fn exit(i32 code) !:
    libc.exit(code)

##:
Gives the process id of the program.

- Returns: The process id, a number greater than 0.
- Example:
  ```sushi
  if (getpid() > 0):
      println("pid ok")
  ```
  This prints `pid ok`.
:##
public fn getpid() i32:
    return libc.getpid()

##:
Gives the real user id of the program.

- Returns: The user id. The C type is unsigned, so a user id of more than 2,147,483,647
  gives a negative number.
- Example:
  ```sushi
  let i32 uid = getuid()
  println("uid {uid}")
  ```
:##
public fn getuid() i32:
    return libc.getuid()

##:
Changes the working directory of the program.

- Parameter path: The new working directory.
- Returns: 0.
- Errors: The `FileError` variant of the errno: `FileError.NotFound` when the path does
  not exist, `FileError.InvalidPath` when a part of the path is not a directory,
  `FileError.PermissionDenied` when the program cannot search the directory.
- Example:
  ```sushi
  match chdir("/no/such/dir"):
      Result.Ok(_) -> println("changed")
      Result.Err(e) -> println("not changed")
  ```
  This prints `not changed`.
:##
public fn chdir(string path) i32 | FileError:
    let i32 rc = libc.chdir(path)
    let i32 code = errno()
    if (rc == 0):
        return Result.Ok(0)
    return Result.Err(FileError.from_errno(code))

##:
Gives the working directory of the program.

The buffer has `PATH_MAX` bytes (from `<sys/platform>`). A longer path is a failure.

- Returns: The absolute path of the working directory.
- Errors: The `FileError` variant of the errno: `FileError.NotFound` when the working
  directory was removed, `FileError.PermissionDenied` when a part of the path cannot be
  read, `FileError.Other` when the path is longer than the buffer.
- Example:
  ```sushi
  match getcwd():
      Result.Ok(path) -> println("in {path}")
      Result.Err(_) -> println("no working directory")
  ```
:##
public fn getcwd() string | FileError:
    let ptr buf = libc.malloc(PATH_MAX as i64)
    let Maybe@(string) path = libc.getcwd(buf, PATH_MAX as i64)
    let i32 code = errno()
    libc.free(buf)
    match nom path:
        Maybe.Some(nom p) -> return Result.Ok(p)
        Maybe.None -> return Result.Err(FileError.from_errno(code))

##:
Reads all the bytes of a capture stream, from the start.

A `tmpfile()` stream can always seek, so a failure of `fseek`, `ftell` or `fread` stops
the program with RE2026.

- Parameter stream: The capture stream.
- Returns: The bytes of the stream, as a string.
:##
fn read_capture(ptr stream) string:
    let i32 at_end = libc.fseek(stream, 0, SEEK_END)
    let i64 size = libc.ftell(stream)
    let i32 at_start = libc.fseek(stream, 0, SEEK_SET)
    assert(at_end == 0 and at_start == 0, "fseek of a capture stream failed")
    assert(size >= 0, "ftell of a capture stream failed")
    let u8[] buf = from([0; size])
    let i64 got = libc.fread(poke buf, 1, size, stream)
    assert(got == size, "fread of a capture stream failed")
    return string.from_bytes(nom buf)

##:
Makes the C argument vector: the command, each argument, and a NULL at the end.

- Parameter cmd: The command, element 0.
- Parameter args: The arguments.
- Returns: A C array of `args.len() + 2` pointers. Free it with `free_argv`.
:##
fn make_argv(string cmd, string[] args) ptr:
    let i64 count = args.len() + 1
    let ptr argv = libc.malloc((count + 1) * 8)
    argv.store_ptr(0, libc.strdup(cmd))
    foreach(i in 0..args.len()):
        argv.store_ptr((i + 1) * 8, libc.strdup(args[i]))
    argv.store_i64(count * 8, 0)
    return argv

##:
Frees a C argument vector that `make_argv` made.

- Parameter argv: The argument vector.
- Parameter count: The count of the strings in it, without the NULL.
:##
fn free_argv(ptr argv, i64 count) ~:
    foreach(i in 0..count):
        match argv.load_ptr(i * 8):
            Maybe.Some(p) -> libc.free(p)
            Maybe.None -> ~
    libc.free(argv)

##:
Waits for a child process. A wait that a signal stops is done again.

- Parameter pid: The process id of the child.
- Returns: The wait status of the child.
:##
fn wait_child(i32 pid) i32:
    let ptr status = libc.malloc(4)
    let i32 rc = libc.waitpid(pid, status, 0)
    let i32 code = errno()
    while (rc == -1 and code == EINTR):
        rc := libc.waitpid(pid, status, 0)
        code := errno()
    let i32 value = status.load_i32(0)
    libc.free(status)
    assert(rc == pid, "waitpid failed")
    return value

##:
Starts a program and gives its exit code, its standard output and its standard error.

The function searches `PATH` for `cmd` (`posix_spawnp`) and gives `cmd` and `args` to the
program as its argument vector, with no shell. The program gets the environment of this
process. Two `tmpfile()` streams hold the standard output and the standard error, so a
program with much output cannot block. A non-zero exit code is no error.

- Parameter cmd: The program, found on `PATH`.
- Parameter args: The arguments, after `cmd`. A bloom `run(cmd, argv...)` lends an array.
- Returns: The `ProcessOutput` of the program.
- Errors: `ProcessError.SpawnFailed` when the program does not start, or when a capture
  stream does not open. `ProcessError.SignalReceived` when a signal stops the program.
- Example:
  ```sushi
  match run("echo", "Mostly Harmless"):
      Result.Ok(out) -> println("{out.exit_code} {out.stdout_text}")
      Result.Err(_) -> println("no output")
  ```
  This prints `0 Mostly Harmless` and a new line.
:##
public fn run(string cmd, ...string args) ProcessOutput | ProcessError:
    let ptr out = libc.tmpfile().or_err(nom ProcessError.SpawnFailed)??
    let Maybe@(ptr) err_stream = libc.tmpfile()
    match err_stream:
        Maybe.None ->
            libc.fclose(out)
            return Result.Err(ProcessError.SpawnFailed)
        Maybe.Some(err) ->
            let i64 count = args.len() + 1
            let ptr argv = make_argv(cmd, args)
            let ptr actions = libc.malloc(FILE_ACTIONS_BYTES)
            let ptr pid = libc.malloc(4)
            libc.posix_spawn_file_actions_init(actions)
            libc.posix_spawn_file_actions_adddup2(actions, libc.fileno(out), 1)
            libc.posix_spawn_file_actions_adddup2(actions, libc.fileno(err), 2)
            let i32 rc = libc.posix_spawnp(pid, cmd, actions, Maybe.None, argv, libc.environ)
            libc.posix_spawn_file_actions_destroy(actions)
            libc.free(actions)
            free_argv(argv, count)
            let i32 child = pid.load_i32(0)
            libc.free(pid)
            if (rc != 0):
                libc.fclose(out)
                libc.fclose(err)
                return Result.Err(ProcessError.SpawnFailed)
            let i32 status = wait_child(child)
            let i32 sig = status & 127
            if (sig != 0 and sig != 127):
                libc.fclose(out)
                libc.fclose(err)
                return Result.Err(ProcessError.SignalReceived)
            let string stdout_text = read_capture(out)
            let string stderr_text = read_capture(err)
            libc.fclose(out)
            libc.fclose(err)
            return Result.Ok(ProcessOutput((status >> 8) & 255, stdout_text, stderr_text))
